<h2>Two buyers, one product</h2>
<p>Guar gum is unusual among our product lines in that it serves two genuinely distinct buyer types under a single HS code. Food manufacturers use it as a natural thickener and stabiliser. Oil and gas companies use industrial-grade guar gum as a key component in hydraulic fracturing (fracking) fluids. Both draw on the same guar seed crop, processed and refined to different purity and viscosity specifications — and both show up in the same US import figures.</p>

<h2>The scale of US demand</h2>
<p>The United States imported USD 95.9 million of Indian guar gum in calendar 2024, roughly 23% of India's USD 417.3 million total guar gum exports worldwide and the clear largest single market — ahead of Russia (USD 65.1 million), Germany (USD 54.9 million) and China (USD 40.1 million). India is the dominant world producer and exporter of guar gum, so these figures represent a large share of total global trade, not just India's share of a market others also supply significantly.</p>

<h2>Food grade vs. industrial grade: specify which you need</h2>
<p>This is the single most important thing to get right on a guar gum enquiry. Food-grade guar gum meets food-safety purity standards for use as a thickener, stabiliser or texturising agent. Industrial-grade guar gum is refined differently, with viscosity performance tuned for drilling-fluid applications, and doesn't carry food-safety certification. They are not interchangeable, and a quote for one isn't comparable to a quote for the other. Tell us your end use upfront.</p>

<h2>Viscosity: the core technical specification</h2>
<p>Viscosity, measured in centipoise (cP) under specified test conditions, is the parameter that determines how guar gum performs in its application — and it's specified differently for food versus industrial use. Mesh size (powder fineness) is a secondary but still important specification affecting how the product disperses and dissolves. Give us your target viscosity range and mesh, and we'll quote against your actual technical requirement.</p>

<h2>Why US demand moves the way it does</h2>
<p>Industrial-grade demand is linked, at least partly, to US oil and gas drilling activity — rig counts and fracking activity levels move with energy prices, which in turn affects guar gum demand and pricing cycles in a way that's disconnected from food-industry demand entirely. If you're a food-grade buyer, understand that guar gum pricing can move for reasons that have nothing to do with food-ingredient markets.</p>

<h2>Documentation</h2>
<p>Standard export documentation applies — phytosanitary certificate, certificate of origin, and a certificate of analysis covering viscosity, moisture and purity. Food-grade buyers should also expect standard FDA food-import documentation on the US import side; industrial-grade shipments typically don't carry that requirement.</p>
